42. Excess Burden of Taxation and the Laffer Curve
Paper: Public Finance (ECNCC303) | Unit: III | Topic: Excess burden of tax – meaning and types, elasticity and excess burden, neutrality, efficiency and equity of taxation, the Laffer curve
Introduction
A tax transfers resources to the government, but it can also impose a loss that no one gains: the excess burden or deadweight loss. Minimising it is central to tax design.
Meaning
Excess burden is the loss of welfare (consumer and producer surplus) beyond the revenue collected, arising because taxes distort choices. A lump-sum tax, unrelated to behaviour, has none.
Types and measurement
• Hicksian approach: the excess burden of a commodity tax equals the extra amount a consumer would pay to be at the same utility as under the tax, minus tax revenue. In the diagram, an indifference curve analysis compares a tax and an income tax of equal yield: the commodity tax puts the consumer on a lower indifference curve.
• Marshallian approach: the triangle under the compensated demand curve: EB = 1/2 x t x dQ.
• Compensating and equivalent variation measure welfare loss.
• Excess burden of income tax: through the labour-leisure choice, the substitution effect reduces work.
Elasticity and excess burden
For small taxes, EB is approximately 1/2 x e x t^2 x Q (where e is the compensated elasticity). Hence:
• It rises with the square of the tax rate, so doubling a tax rate quadruples its excess burden. Broad bases and low rates are best.
• It rises with the elasticity of demand or supply.
This underlies the Ramsey rule: tax inelastic goods more.
Neutrality, efficiency and equity
A neutral tax does not distort relative prices or decisions, and a non-neutral one distorts. Efficiency demands minimal excess burden and low administration costs. Equity involves the benefit principle and ability-to-pay principle (horizontal and vertical equity). Efficiency and equity trade off: progressive taxes are equitable but have higher distortions on labour and savings.
The Laffer curve
Arthur Laffer suggested a relationship between tax rate and revenue: at 0 per cent and 100 per cent tax rates, revenue is zero; revenue rises as rates increase up to a maximum at some rate t*, and then falls as the tax base shrinks due to reduced work, evasion and avoidance. Beyond t*, cutting rates raises revenue (the prohibitive range). Policy influence: supply-side economics, Reagan tax cuts. Evidence suggests most countries are on the rising side; t* is uncertain (estimates 50-70 per cent), and tax cuts typically do not pay for themselves. Critics point out the difficulty of estimating the peak, and the role of base and enforcement.
Conclusion
Excess burden shows that taxes carry hidden costs, and the Laffer curve warns about extreme rates. Good tax design uses broad bases, moderate rates and attention to elasticities.